  "preamble": "The deletion of the velar nasal /ŋ/ in word-initial position is a documented sound change across unrelated language families, driven by the cross-linguistic rarity and articulatory difficulty of word-initial /ŋ/ in many phonological systems. It is attested in ongoing change in Hong Kong Cantonese, where the onset /ŋ/ is merging with a zero onset. Initial-consonant loss is also a hallmark of several Pama-Nyungan subgroups in Australia, including Northern Paman (Uradhi ani < *nga:ni 'what') and Arandic (Arrernte aye- < *ngayu 'I'), and Arabana dropped /ŋ/- within the historical record.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Nasal Deletion, Lenition, Onset Reduction",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Hong Kong Cantonese",
      "languageFamily": "Sinitic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "我 ngo5 [ŋɔː˨˧] 'I, me'",
          "to": "o5 [ɔː˨˧]",
          "note": "Onset /ŋ/ is lost in light-tone syllables in younger speakers' speech, merging with the zero onset; prescriptivists stigmatize it as 'lazy pronunciation' (懶音)."
        },
        {
          "from": "牛 ngau4 [ŋɐu4] 'cow'",
          "to": "[ɐu4]"
        }
      ]
    },
